If PythonPrograms is your Git repository, then you should not create the worktree inside this folder.

Recommended Structure

Suppose your repository is:

E:\
└── AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS\
    ├── PythonPrograms       ← Main Git repository
    ├── python1              ← Worktree 1
    ├── python2              ← Worktree 2
    └── python3              ← Worktree 3
Step 1: Open Terminal in PythonPrograms
cd E:\AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS\PythonPrograms
Step 2: Create a new worktree
git worktree add -b feature/python1 ..\python1

Here:

feature/python1 → New branch name
..\python1 → Creates a folder named python1 alongside PythonPrograms

After running the command:

E:\
└── AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS\
    ├── PythonPrograms
    ├── python1

Open the python1 folder in PyCharm or VS Code, and you'll have another working copy of the repository on the feature/python1 branch.

Create another worktree
git worktree add -b feature/python2 ..\python2

Now your structure becomes:

E:\
└── AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS\
    ├── PythonPrograms      (main branch)
    ├── python1             (feature/python1)
    └── python2             (feature/python2)
Verify

Run:

git worktree list

Example output:

E:/AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS/PythonPrograms  a1b2c3d [main]
E:/AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS/python1         e4f5g6h [feature/python1]
E:/AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS/python2         i7j8k9l [feature/python2]


After Git Worktree

Instead of switching branches, open each worktree as a separate project.

Suppose your folders are:

E:\AUTOMATION_FRAMEWORKS\
│
├── PythonPrograms      ← main
├── python1             ← feature/python1
└── python2             ← feature/python2
Open all three folders

Open three IDE windows.

Window 1
PythonPrograms

Current branch:

main
Window 2
python1

Current branch:

feature/python1

Verify:

git branch

Output:

* feature/python1
Window 3
python2

