Each terminal is completely independent.

Let's do a small exercise
In feature/python1

Create:

MathOperations.py
def square(n):
    return n * n

Commit it.

In feature/python2

Create:

StringUtilities.py
def reverse(text):
    return text[::-1]

Commit it.

Now run:

git log --oneline --graph --all

You'll see both branches have different commit histories, proving they're developing independently.

Think of it like this
                    Git Repository
                          │
        ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
        │                 │                 │
        ▼                 ▼                 ▼
   PythonPrograms      python1          python2
      (main)      (feature/python1) (feature/python2)

      IDE 1            IDE 2             IDE 3
      Terminal 1       Terminal 2        Terminal 3

     Build/Test       Build/Test        Build/Test

This is the primary advantage of Git Worktrees: each worktree is an independent working directory with its own checked-out branch, 
so you can edit, run, test, commit, and push changes in parallel without ever switching branches.

